window.REEF_DOCS = {"methods_md": "# METHODS_3D - Borth coastal defence reef (Phase 1, Ceredigion, Wales)\n\n3D model v1, 2026-10-07 (3D agent, Sonnet 5.5); seabed fix D6 applied 2026-10-07 (3.3; model v1.1; the pre-fix files are in `3d\\archive\\pre_D6\\`). Files: `3d\\model.js` (data), `3d\\index.html` (viewer), `3d\\build_3d.py` (rebuilds model.js, docs.js and, with `--shape`, shape.json), `3d\\make_annotations.py` and `3d\\nav_read.py` (annotated images), `3d\\SOURCES_3D.md` (step-by-step log). Not for navigation.\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThe model shows the **as-built final layout of Borth Phase 1 (completed 8 March 2012)**: a northern boot-shaped **surf reef** (crest +0.50 mODN, tail +1.00, head edge +0.00 rising over about 40 m) and a southern oval that is a **shore-parallel breakwater, not a surf reef** (crest +1.50 mODN), on a seabed rebuilt from the 2022 LiDAR beach, the HRPP576 Fig 2 contours, the design bed levels and the EMODnet trend, with the water surface selectable from LAT to MHWS. Three outlines of the same structure are modelled and selectable: the **laser-survey edge (Welsh Government LiDAR, 19 Mar 2022, 8,675 m2, default, chosen by Lior on 2026-10-06)**, the design rock-layer foot of drawing 9V5090/1020 (Jan 2011, 6,989 m2) and the visible rock edge in the Esri photo of 17 Sep 2024 (5,743 m2). The default surface is the 1 m LiDAR DSM above the exposed level (about -2.3 mODN) plus the design toe berm and blanket of drawings 1021-1023 below it; the model rock volume is 30,100 m3 (about 51,200 t at 1.7 t/m3, +-15 %). No later damage, deflation or removal was found (imagery 2012-2024, 2022 LiDAR), so no second state is modelled. **3D confidence: MEDIUM**: the plan and the crest levels are well supported (laser survey and design agree within 0.3 m), but the seabed under and around the rock rests on a design bed level (about -4.0 mODN, \"bed levels vary\"), a hand-read contour figure and an estimated MSL offset, and the sea floor seaward of the reef now follows the Fig 2 -4.0 contour (fixed on 2026-10-07: it had been 0.89 m deeper than that contour, sections 3.3 and 4.4), so heights above the bed are known to about +-0.5 m and volumes to about +-15 %.\n\n## 2. Data sources\n\nSource audit 2026-10-10: the full register (what was read off each source, how, accuracy, model element), the methods list, assumptions A1-A14 with effect and replacement, decisions DEC1-DEC14 and confidence per element are in `REGISTER_3D.md` (viewer: Methods tab, second button). The table below is the original source list; the registry now has 31 rows (img-31 = Blacka et al. 2013, Fig 4.1).\n\nCitations are author-year; \"id\" is the source id used in `model.js` provenance and `shape.json`. Registry ids refer to `03_images\\reefs\\borth-coastal-defence-reef\\images.json`. All local copies are private research copies; reuse rights are to be checked before any public release.\n\n| id | citation | contributed | resolution / date |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| drg1020-1023 | Haskoning UK Ltd for Ceredigion County Council (2010-11). *Borth Coastal Protection Scheme - Phase 1*, drawings 9V5090/1020, /1021, /1022, /1023 rev C1 (For Construction, Jan 2011). http://www.borthcommunity.info/index.php/planning-drawings/65-planning-drawings, accessed 2026-10-06 (registry img-20..23) | plan rings, 15 setting-out points (OS grid), crest levels +0.50 / +1.00 / +0.00 / +1.50, layers (Type 4 2.70 m, Type 5, Type 6 0.5 m), toe berm (1.35 m, 3 m top, 1:1.5), slopes 1:3 / 1:4 / 1:5, tide levels printed (MHWS +2.56, MLWS -1.74), bed note \"-4.2 to -3.6m AOD\" | vector PDF, 1:500 plan, 1:200 sections; georeference residual 0.02 m |\n| lidar2022 | Welsh Government (2022). *LiDAR 2020-22, 1 m DSM and DTM, tile SN6089*, flown 2022-03-19 03:15-03:20 UTC. DataMapWales; Open Government Licence. https://dmwproductionblob.blob.core.windows.net/lidar-zips/2020-22/dsm/wg_del_29_260289_20220319dsm.tif (DTM: same path with dtm), accessed 2026-10-06 (registry img-24) | as-built exposed surface (DSM), beach and foreshore (DTM), water edge about -2.3 mODN | 1 m, EPSG:27700, vertical datum not stated (ODN assumed, A4) |\n| hrpp576f2 | Rigden, T., Stewart, T., Allsop, W. and Johnson, A. (2013). *Surf reefs - physical modelling results, not pipe dreams*. HR Wallingford report HRPP576; ICE Coasts, Marine Structures and Breakwaters, Edinburgh, Sept 2013, Fig 2. https://eprints.hrwallingford.com/931/1/HRPP576_SurfReefs.pdf, accessed 2026-10-06 (registry img-17) | pre-construction contours +0.0 ... -5.0 mODN around the reefs (traced -2, -3, -4); oval C position; 500 m scale bar | figure 849 x 349 px, 1.776 m/px, +-10 m |\n| sat2024 | Esri World Imagery (2024-09-17, also 2012-10-27, 2013-06-04, 2022-08-25), zoom 19, 5.4995 px/m. https://server.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/World_Imagery/MapServer, accessed 2026-10-06 (registry img-08..11) | traced visible-rock outline (version sat_2024), waterline height at the traced edge | +-5 m absolute |\n| emodnet | EMODnet Bathymetry Consortium (2024). *EMODnet Digital Bathymetry (DTM 2024)*. https://doi.org/10.12770/cf51df64-56f9-4a99-b1aa-36b8d7b743a1 (CC BY 4.0); survey cells CDI 115084 (OceanWise Ltd), accessed 2026-10-06 (registry img-14..16) | offshore gradient -1.1 % from y = 335 m outwards; reef-zone cells are GEBCO interpolation (1.0-1.4 m too shallow) and are not used | 1/16 arc-minute cells, one sounding per cell, age > 30 y |\n| ref3 | Royal HaskoningDHV (2011). *West of Wales Shoreline Management Plan 2*, Section 4 Coastal Area C Introduction, p. 4C.3, water-level table (Aberystwyth). https://www.grwparfordirolgorllewincymru.cymru/sites/default/files/2019-06/4c1%20-%20Section%204%20Coastal%20Area%20C%20Introduction.pdf, accessed 2026-10-06 | MHWS +2.56, MHWN +1.06, MLWN -0.64, MLWS -1.74 mAOD (= mODN) | tabulated |\n| ref4 | National Tidal and Sea Level Facility (n.d.). *Chart datum and ordnance datum: differences at selected ports*. https://ntslf.org/tides/datum, accessed 2026-10-06 | LAT = chart datum = ODN - 2.44 m at Barmouth and Fishguard (Aberystwyth not listed) | +-0.1 m |\n| ref6 | Hansford, M. (Mark) (2011). Borth's Big Dig. *New Civil Engineer*, 15 Sept 2011. https://www.newcivilengineer.com/archive/borths-big-dig-15-09-2011/ | 275,000 t rock for the whole Phase 1, Type 4 (6-10 t) 42,000 t | upper bound only |\n| ref7 | RCAHMW (2020). Coflein, Coastal Defences, Borth, NPRN 424699. https://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/424699, accessed 2026-10-06 | completion 8 March 2012, reef about 300 m offshore | - |\n| navionics | Garmin Ltd / Navionics (2026). Marine Maps web viewer (Nautical Charts and SonarChart Maps), \"Not to be used for navigation\". https://maps.garmin.com/en-US/marine?maps=another-brand&overlay=false&key=gcm4edq678dp (successor of webapp.navionics.com), accessed 2026-10-07 (registry rows added this run) | cross-check of the seabed and datum (section 3.6); NOT used as a model input | zoom 17 / 18, 0.73 / 0.36 m/px; contour interval 0.5 m shallow |\n\nNot found at a primary source (searched): the highest astronomical tide (HAT) at Aberystwyth; a reef-only rock volume or tonnage; an as-built plan or post-construction survey of the reef; a Navionics/SonarChart depth for the reef itself.\n\n## 3. Methods\n\n### 3.1 Frame and datums\n\n* Canonical frame of `shape.json` (metres): x alongshore, + toward SOUTH (bearing 179.57 deg); y offshore, + toward WEST (bearing 269.57 deg); origin at the foot of the perpendicular from the centroid of both mounds on the defence line (back of the shingle beach, bearing 359.57 deg). z up.\n* OS grid to canonical is an affine map fitted over a 600 x 600 m window to the exact chain (OSGB36 -> WGS84 Helmert via pyproj -> local tangent plane -> rotation): residual rms 0.005 m, max 0.02 m (`b3d_lib.Frame`). Absolute position is limited by the Helmert (+-2-5 m), not by this fit.\n* Vertical: working datum Ordnance Datum Newlyn (ODN = mAOD). Model zero = mean sea level:\n\n```\nz_MSL = z_ODN - 0.31                     (E1)  MSL = +0.31 mODN = (MLWS + MLWN + MHWN + MHWS)/4, +-0.15 m  (A2)\nz_LAT = z_ODN + 2.44      (depth below chart datum d_CD = -(z_ODN + 2.44) for the sea floor)   (E2)  LAT = -2.44 mODN (A3)\nz_MSL = e_LAT - 2.75                     (E3)  EMODnet cells are given relative to LAT\n```\n\n* Tide levels (mODN / m MSL): LAT -2.44 / -2.75; MLWS -1.74 / -2.05; MLWN -0.64 / -0.95; MSL +0.31 / 0; MHWN +1.06 / +0.75; MHWS +2.56 / +2.25. **The slider stops at MHWS** because the HAT of Aberystwyth was not found at a primary source (the only HAT-like figure, 5.9 m chart datum, is a predicted maximum, not HAT), and MHWS is the highest level with a published source (ref3; also printed on drawing 9V5090/1021). Two photo/flight levels are offered as extra presets: LiDAR flight 19 Mar 2022 (about -2.3 mODN, read as the sea surface at the rock edge) and the Esri photo of 17 Sep 2024 (about -1.3 mODN, median DSM height along the traced edge).\n* No Haifa/Israel tide preset (Lior's rule): local tides only.\n\n### 3.2 Reef surface, three versions (1 m grid, window x -105..150, y 235..420 m)\n\nCommon design constants (drawings 1021-1023): crest +0.50 (arm, 9 m wide), +1.00 (tail, strip R2/R5-R3/R4, transition 15 m from SOP R2), +0.00 at the head edge (R1/R10) rising to +0.50 over about 40 m, +1.50 (oval, flat top 6 m x 38-46 m between R14 and R15); Type 4 layer 2.70 m thick; toe berm 1.35 m high, 3 m top, 1:1.5 sides; flat apron 2.0 m; blanket 0.5 m with 1:1.5 edge; foot of the Type 4 slope = top of the toe berm = **-2.15 mODN** (bed -4.0 + blanket 0.5 + berm 1.35).\n\n* **design_1020** (Royal Haskoning rock-layer foot). Concentric rings 0-4 of drawing 1020 are extracted from the vector PDF and georeferenced with the 15 setting-out points. Between rings the surface is interpolated linearly in the distance to the two bounding rings: ring 0 (apron outer edge) at the seabed -> ring 1 (blanket top edge, +0.5 m) -> ring 2 -> ring 3 (berm top outer edge) -> ring 4 at -2.15; inside ring 4 the surface rises linearly in distance to the crest outline (R1-R6, R2-R5 paths, crest zones with the heights above) at the crest height of the zone. The 1:3 / 1:4 / 1:5 slopes are therefore not imposed separately; they result from ring spacing (the plan is drawn to those slopes). Surface = max(design, seabed).\n* **lidar_2022** (default). Inside the outline of the exposed rock (cells with a LiDAR return, water edge about -2.3 mODN, 1 m cells, simplified 0.4 m) the surface is the LiDAR DSM, lightly smoothed (normalised Gaussian, sigma 0.8 m, A9) and never below the edge level; outside the outline the design toe is attached with the **same toe slopes as drawings 1021-1023**: 1:1.5 down from the edge level to the blanket top (bed + 0.5 m), 2 m flat apron, 1:1.5 blanket edge to the seabed (A11). The surface below the water edge is therefore design, not measured.\n* **sat_2024** (visible rock edge). The same construction with the 79-vertex outline traced on the 2024 Esri image and edge level -1.3 mODN. Because the toe slope is imposed from the edge (1:1.5) it is steeper than the real flanks and the volume is smaller.\n* Zones for colour: 1 armour / surveyed surface (Type 4), 2 toe berm (Type 0), 3 blanket and apron (Type 6).\n* Rock volume is the integral of (surface - modelled seabed) over the 1 m grid, both mounds, including berm and blanket. Type-4-layer volume is the integral of min(2.70 m, surface - bed) over the armour zone. Tonnes = volume x 1.7 t/m3 (A8).\n\n### 3.3 Seabed\n\nThin-plate-spline radial basis function (scipy `RBFInterpolator`, smoothing 0.5, 80 nearest neighbours) through control nodes in the canonical frame, evaluated on a 5 m grid (x -200..260, y 0..520 m); the nodes are drawn by source in `annotated\\A8_seabed_source_zones.png`:\n\n| nodes | source | level | note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 3,081 | LiDAR DTM 19 Mar 2022, beach and lower foreshore, 6 m grid, y < 255 m, rock buffered out by 12 m, only cells above -2.35 mODN | measured | water edge reached about y 245 m |\n| 12 | HRPP576 Fig 2 -3.0 mODN contour, outside the reef zone (x not in -115..+160) | -3.0 | read by eye, +-0.5 m, +-10 m horizontally |\n| 13 | HRPP576 Fig 2 -4.0 mODN contour, traced points outside the reef zone (x -200..-118 and x 170; **added 2026-10-07, D6**) | -4.0 | read by eye, +-0.5 m, +-10 m horizontally; y 408-420 m west of the reef, 460 m at x 170 |\n| 6 | ASSUMED -4.0 bridge nodes at x -64, -24, 16, 56, 96, 136 on the straight line between the traced ends (-104, 420) and (170, 460), where the contour is hidden behind the drawn mounds of the figure (**D6**) | -4.0 | assumed (interpolation); stops the thin-plate fit from overshooting between the design bed and the contour |\n| 3 | ASSUMED -4.0 nodes east of the traced end of the contour: (200, 464), (230, 469), (260, 473), the trend of the last 270 m (+0.146 m/m); the traced contour leaves the figure at y about 460 m, x about 170 (**D6**) | -4.0 | assumed (extrapolation); 70-140 m east of the reef |\n| 12 + 3 | design bed under the north arm (drawing 1021 N3: 2.70 + 1.31 + 0.50 below +0.50 = -4.01) and N4 (-3.8) | -4.0 / -3.8 | derived from layer thicknesses; the section ground line is schematic (\"EXISTING SEABED VARIES\") |\n| 6 | tail: ASSUMED -3.0 (y 272) and -3.4 (y 282), shallower than the arm | assumed | no drawing value |\n| 10 | oval: printed note on S2 \"-4.2 TO -3.6m AOD\": -3.6 shoreward (y 309), -4.2 seaward (y 340) | -3.6 / -4.2 | |\n\nBeyond the Fig 2 -4.0 contour, y_c(x) (the traced points, joined by straight lines across the gap x -104..170 and continued east by the 3 assumed nodes), the level fitted on the contour is continued with the EMODnet gradient (A7):\n\n```\nz(x, y) = z_RBF(x, y_c(x)) - 0.011 (y - y_c(x))   for y > y_c(x)   (E4)       (-1.1 %, soundings y 368-652 m, CDI 115084; y_c = 408-473 m)\n```\n\nThe RBF reproduces all nodes (rms < 0.01 m). Under the footprints the modelled bed is -3.86 mean (-4.05..-2.51) under the north foot ring and -3.95 (-4.21..-3.62) under the oval (design -4.0 and -3.6..-4.2: met; the mean under the north ring is shallower than -4.0 because the tail bed is assumed shallower, -3.0..-3.4).\n\n**D6 fix, 2026-10-07 (bug fix of the seabed outside the reef; model v1 -> v1.1).** Two faults of the first build are removed:\n\n1. *Cause.* `seabed_grid` evaluated the thin-plate fit at `min(y, 335)` (the former ANCHOR_Y) and subtracted the EMODnet gradient beyond it. Every node with y > 335 m was therefore ignored: the design bed nodes under the head of the north arm (y 360 and 385, -4.0) and the oval seaward nodes had no effect there, and the bed under the arm head fell to -4.0 - 0.011 (y - 335) = -4.7 mODN at y 397. The first build also relied on an assumed anchor line (-4.0 at y = 335 m outside x -80..130) and used the Fig 2 -4.0 contour only as a check, so the sea floor in front of the reef was 0.89 m (sd 0.14, n 13) deeper than that contour (-4.7 at y 400, -5.1 at y 440, where Fig 2 has -4.0 at y 408-460).\n2. *Fix.* The anchor nodes are removed; the Fig 2 -4.0 contour (13 traced points, plus 6 bridge and 3 extension nodes marked ASSUMED above) is a control; the gradient E4 starts on that contour instead of at y = 335 m. The fit now uses the design bed nodes at every y.\n\n*Effect, old -> new (model.js, 1 m grid; the only change is the bed level, plan and surface are untouched):*\n\n| quantity | before D6 | after D6 |\n|---|---|---|\n| model minus Fig 2 -4.0 contour (n 13) | -0.89 m (sd 0.14) | 0.00 m (sd 0.00) |\n| model minus Fig 2 -3.0 contour (n 24, control) | -0.01 m | 0.00 m |\n| model minus Fig 2 -2.0 contour (n 24, check) | -0.14 m | -0.14 m (unchanged) |\n| model minus EMODnet cells at x -30, y 368 / 440 / 513 | -1.52 / -1.62 / -1.61 m (mean -1.58) | -1.16 / -0.73 / -0.72 m (mean -0.87) |\n| chart zero implied by the model (Navionics, section 3.6) | -4.73 +- 0.20 mODN (RMS vs LAT 2.30 m) | -3.75 +- 0.22 mODN (RMS vs LAT 1.33 m) |\n| bed under the north foot ring (mean, range) | -4.04 (-4.72..-2.51) | -3.86 (-4.05..-2.51) |\n| rock volume lidar_2022 (default) | 31,336 m3 = 53,271 t | 30,119 m3 = 51,203 t (-3.9 %) |\n| rock volume design_1020 | 30,091 m3 = 51,154 t | 28,919 m3 = 49,162 t (-3.9 %) |\n| rock volume sat_2024 | 26,400 m3 = 44,880 t | 25,504 m3 = 43,357 t (-3.4 %) |\n| Type 4 layer, default | 21,557 m3 = 36,646 t | 21,220 m3 = 36,074 t (-1.6 %) |\n\n*Why the volumes changed, and why that is accepted.* The brief for the fix asked for unchanged volumes, on the belief that the design nodes already pinned the bed under the rock. They did not: the nodes at y > 335 m were ignored (fault 1), so the bed under the head of the arm (y 335-397 m) was up to 0.7 m too deep and the total volumes were overstated by 3.4-3.9 %. The coordinator therefore replaced the rule \"volumes unchanged\" by \"volumes change only through the bed-node fix, documented\" (2026-10-07). *Option A (rejected):* freeze the bed under the rock at the old values to keep the numbers. It would keep a pit under the arm head, up to 0.7 m deeper than both the design bed (-4.0) that the documentation states and the new sea floor around it. Option B (applied) makes the model agree with its own documentation. The bed under the rock is still a design level (+-0.4 m, section 5); the volume uncertainty (E9) is unchanged in kind.\n\n*New residuals to know.* (a) The design bed under the arm (-4.0 at y 310-385) is 0.2-0.6 m deeper than a straight interpolation of the Fig 2 contours at the same place (-3.0 at y about 290, -4.0 at y about 415: -3.4 at y 335, -3.8 at y 385); the fit lets the bed rise from the design level to the Fig 2 level within about 40 m of the reef zone. Both are inside +-0.5 m. (b) The model is still 0.7-1.2 m deeper than the EMODnet absolute cells at y 368-513 (the cells are GEBCO-interpolated and are not used) and its implied Navionics zero (-3.75) is 0.17 m deeper than that implied by the Fig 2 -4.0 contour (-3.58). (c) The -4.0 level between x -104 and 170 and east of x 170 is bridged / extended by assumption (9 nodes); it lies 70 m or more from the rock. (d) A small ridge of 0.05-0.18 m per 5 m cell at y 340-345, x 160-260, comes from the oval nodes (-4.2 at y 340); it is inside the bed uncertainty.\n\n### 3.4 Tides, water surface, viewer\n\nThe water is a horizontal plane at the selected level (static still water; no wave set-up, run-up or surf). The viewer shows seabed (coloured by depth), reef (coloured by zone), the water plane at 38 % opacity, a 10 m grid (50 m bold), 10 m and 50 m scale bars, a north arrow, the defence line y = 0, an outline of the selected version at its edge level, and a camera read-out (position, true heading, pitch, field of view). Vertical exaggeration 1-5 x (default 1) multiplies z only and is always shown; presets: oblique from the sea, plan (north to the left), along the surf-reef crest, from the beach at eye height 1.7 m, side view. Photo-match mode: a local image is overlaid on the canvas and camera x, y, z, true heading, pitch and field of view are adjusted until the rock outlines line up; \"Copy camera\" prints the parameters (vertical exaggeration forced to 1). Version selector with an (i) pop-up (`versions_info` plus a table name | date | source | footprint | volume); `#version=<id>` in the URL selects a version; `#water=`, `#ve=`, `#view=` also work.\n\n### 3.5 Assumptions\n\n* A1 The drawn design is the built design (no as-built plan was published); the layout is the final one completed on 8 March 2012 (ref7); the superseded two-geotube design is mentioned only.\n* A2 MSL = +0.31 mODN (+-0.15). A3 LAT = -2.44 mODN (+-0.1; from Barmouth and Fishguard, MHWS 5.00 m CD and the LiDAR sea surface at a spring low).\n* A4 LiDAR heights are ODN (not stated in the tile); supported by the agreement with the design crests (oval p90 +1.75 vs +1.50 design, a block-top effect) and the sea surface at the flight (-2.2..-2.4 vs LAT -2.44).\n* A5 The LiDAR edge level (water at the flight) is -2.3 mODN (+-0.1), inferred from the data, not from a tide prediction.\n* A6 Seabed under the rock: arm -4.0 (derived), tail -3.0 .. -3.4 (assumed), N4 -3.8, oval -3.6 / -4.2 (printed note). Derived bed under the oval centreline from S1-S1 is -3.4, 0.2 m shallower than the printed range.\n* A7 The EMODnet gradient -1.1 % is applied beyond the Fig 2 -4.0 mODN contour, starting from the fitted level on the contour (D6, 2026-10-07; replaces the first build's assumed anchor -4.0 at y = 335 m, an orchestrator decision of 2026-10-06 that was reversed on 2026-10-07 because it left the model 0.89 m deeper than the contour). The -4.0 contour is bridged across x -104..170 and extended east of x 170 by assumption (9 nodes).\n* A8 Placed-rock bulk density 1.7 t/m3 (range 1.6-1.8; solid 2.65 t/m3, voids about 36 %).\n* A9 The DSM is smoothed with sigma 0.8 m; the DSM shows block tops, 0.1-0.3 m above the design surface.\n* A10 Edge levels of the versions: -2.3 (lidar_2022), -2.15 (design_1020), -1.3 (sat_2024; median DSM height along the traced edge: north -1.25, south -1.36 mODN).\n* A11 Flanks below the water edge are design (1:1.5 berm and blanket slopes, 2 m apron); the as-built flanks above the edge are flatter than the design (LiDAR 1:4.0-1:5.3 vs 1:3) and 8-10 m wider; below the edge they are unknown.\n* A12 Still, horizontal water surface at each tide level.\n* A13 The Navionics chart datum is LAT (UK convention); the viewer does not state it.\n\n### 3.6 Navionics chart reading and datum test\n\n*Access.* The old Navionics ChartViewer (webapp.navionics.com) now redirects to Garmin's Marine Maps viewer. In my own headless Chrome (a random free DevTools port, a fresh temporary profile, process terminated by PID afterwards; never the shared browser pane) the page loaded without login, CAPTCHA or consent dialog; nothing was accepted or bypassed. The map was centred on the centroid of the two mounds (52.483453 N, 4.056205 W) with the page's geohash key; options Depth units = Metres; chart types SonarChart Maps and Nautical Charts; zoom 17 and 18 (maximum); \"Shallow shading\" 0, 1, ... 10 m (44 screenshots, `src\\navionics\\`, `capture_navionics.py`, `capture_log.json`). The viewer does **not** state a datum or a contour interval and shows no data dates; screenshots are private research copies (\"Not to be used for navigation\", Garmin Navionics).\n\n*Geometry.* The screenshot is the 982 x 655 px map container, north up; the page itself reports the centre pixel (491, 327). Pixel to ground:\n\n```\nr = 156543.03392 / 2^zoom   (Mercator m/px; 0.730 m/px of ground at zoom 17)      (E5)\n(col,row) -> Mercator (x_c + (col-491) r, y_c - (row-327) r) -> WGS84 -> OS grid -> canonical (E6)\n```\n\n*Reading.* Along 8 pixel rows (x from -194 to +198 m) thin dark runs are contour lines; counted outward from the edge of the green \"drying\" area (the 0 m line) they are 0, 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2, 2.5 m (the printed labels 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2, 2.5 confirm the 0.5 m interval). The 0 m line is at y = 385 (x -56) to 430 m (x +198), then 0.5 m at +45 m, 1 m at +105, 1.5 m at +163, 2 m at +212, 2.5 m at +293 m farther offshore. The Nautical Chart layer shows only the 0 m and 2 m lines and one drying-height label (0.2 m at x -108.5, y 248). `annotated\\A9_navionics_sonarchart_z17_annotated.png`, `A10_navionics_nautical_z18_reef_outline.png`.\n\n*Datum test.* If a reference gives the seabed level z_ref (mODN) at a point where the chart gives depth d below its datum, the chart zero is z_ref + d (mODN). Zero levels implied by each reference (mean +- sd, n):\n\n| reference | implied chart zero (mODN) | RMS vs LAT -2.44 | MLWS -1.74 | MLWN -0.64 | MSL +0.31 | ODN 0 | MHWN +1.06 | MHWS +2.56 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| HRPP576 Fig 2 -4.0 contour, 7 points x -194..+169 | -3.58 +- 0.07 (7) | **1.15** | 1.84 | 2.94 | 3.89 | 3.58 | 4.64 | 6.14 |\n| EMODnet cells at x -31 (y 368, 440, 513) | -3.07 +- 0.08 (3) | **0.64** | 1.34 | 2.43 | 3.38 | 3.07 | 4.13 | 5.63 |\n| this model's seabed (24 line crossings, y <= 515; after D6) | -3.75 +- 0.22 (24) | **1.33** | 2.02 | 3.12 | 4.07 | 3.76 | 4.82 | 6.32 |\n\nLAT is the best standard datum against all three references and the one drying-height label agrees with it (0.2 m above chart datum = -2.24 mODN; the LiDAR has water there at -2.3: within 0.1 m), so the chart datum is taken as LAT (A13). But no datum fits well offshore: the charted 0 m line lies 140-180 m seaward of the LiDAR water edge (y about 245 m at -2.3 mODN) and the chart is 0.6-1.1 m shallower than the Fig 2 and EMODnet references (and 1.3 m shallower than this model, 2.3 m before the D6 fix); SonarChart contours are partly interpolated and their survey dates are not shown. The chart is therefore a cross-check only and was **not** used to build the seabed.\n\n*Crest and height.* The structure still exists (LiDAR 2022; imagery to 2024) but it is **not resolved** by either layer: the rock mounds (0 to +1.5 mODN, 2.4-4 m above LAT) lie inside the uniformly green drying area with no symbol, label or contour at the footprint. The only charted shoal (closed 0.5 and 1 m loops) is at canonical (37, 405), 35 m seaward of the nearest rock, and is not the reef. Navionics therefore gives no crest depth or height, and no cross-check of the design figures was possible.\n\n## 4. Validation\n\n### 4.1 The three outline versions compared\n\n| | lidar_2022 (default) | design_1020 | sat_2024 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| name | Laser survey edge (Welsh Government LiDAR, 19 Mar 2022) | Design rock-layer foot (Royal Haskoning drawing 9V5090/1020 rev C1, Jan 2011) | Visible rock edge (Esri satellite photo, 17 Sep 2024) |\n| kind / date | laser survey, 2022-03-19 | design drawing, 2011-01 | photo trace, 2024-09-17 |\n| edge definition | rock exposed at the water level of the flight, about -2.3 mODN | foot of the Type 4 armour = top of the toe berm, -2.15 mODN | rock above the waterline in the photo, about -1.3 mODN |\n| footprint at the edge | 8,675 m2 (207 x 150 m bbox) | 6,989 m2 (197 x 144 m) | 5,743 m2 (196 x 140 m) |\n| footprint incl. toe and blanket | 11,391 m2 | 11,491 m2 | 8,865 m2 |\n| surface | LiDAR DSM above the edge + design toe below | design (rings 0-4 + crest zones) | LiDAR DSM above the edge + design toe (1:1.5) below |\n| rock volume above the modelled seabed (after D6; before: 31,336 / 30,091 / 26,400 m3) | 30,119 m3 (+-4,556) | 28,919 m3 (+-4,596) | 25,504 m3 (+-3,546) |\n| tonnes at 1.7 t/m3 (range 1.6-1.8) | 51,200 (48,200-54,200) | 49,200 (46,300-52,100) | 43,400 (40,800-45,900) |\n| Type 4 armour layer (2.70 m) | 21,220 m3 = 36,100 t | 17,795 m3 = 30,300 t | 15,366 m3 = 26,100 t |\n\nThe footprints differ because the three edges sit at different heights on the same sloping flanks (-2.3, -2.15, -1.3 mODN at about 1:4-1:5), not because the structure moved (no change was found in the imagery 2012-2024: the 2022 outline lies within 0.8 m of the 2024 one; the 5 m offset of the 2012 image is registration). The volumes differ much less than the footprints: the default and the design agree within 4 % in total volume, the photo version is 15 % lower because its edge is high and the imposed 1:1.5 toe is steep, and the Type 4 layer volume rises from 15.4k to 21.2k m3 as the edge moves down. In every version the Type 4 layer (26-36 kt) stays below the 42,000 t of Type 4 rock that NCE (ref6) gives for the **whole** Phase 1 (breakwaters, groynes and revetment included), so the model is consistent with that upper bound. No reef-only quantity exists at a primary source.\n\n### 4.2 Crest and shape against the laser survey\n\n(`annotated\\A6_lidar_crest_check.png`.) LiDAR DSM inside the design crest strips: north arm median +0.58, p90 +0.91 mODN (design +0.50); oval median +1.55, p90 +1.75 (design +1.50); the model max crest is +1.81 (DSM block tops). On the common cells above -2.0 mODN the default surface minus the design surface is +0.14 m mean, sd 0.28 m. Along the arm axis the DSM follows the design steps (+0.0 -> +0.5 over about 40 m, +0.5 -> +1.0 over about 15 m). Across the oval the DSM top agrees (+1.5) and the flanks are 8-10 m wider than the design 1:3 (as-built slope 1:4.0-1:4.1). The earlier verification quotes N arm median +0.69 on a different cell mask; the difference (0.11 m) is the mask, not a conflict.\n\n### 4.3 Geometry checks\n\n* Frame fit rms 0.005 m (max 0.02 m); design foot ring (ring 4) reproduced from the PDF against `shape.json` alt_outlines: IoU 0.9998; the PDF-to-OS-grid fit of drawing 1020 has maximum residual 0.12 pt (0.02 m); the re-projected rings, crest paths and setting-out points lie on the drawing to about 1 pixel (`A2_drg1020_rings_and_sop_read.png`).\n* Section readings on drawings 1021-1023 (`A3`-`A5`): arm crest +0.50, Type 4 underside -2.20, head +0.00 / -2.70, tail +1.00 / -1.70, oval +1.50 / -1.20 / -2.90, toe berm 1350 mm, apron 2000, blanket 500; MHWS +2.56 and MLWS -1.74 printed; transitions 40 m and 15 m; oval S1-S1 is dimensioned 23,000 + 23,000 mm between SOP R15 and R14 (46 m), whereas the setting-out table of drawing 1020 puts R14 and R15 38 m apart (the model uses the table; the strip measured on the plan is 38 m): an unresolved inconsistency inside the drawing set, worth about 2 % of the oval volume.\n\n### 4.4 Seabed checks\n\n* Under the footprints: model -3.86 mean under the north foot ring (design -4.0; shallower because of the assumed tail bed) and -3.95 under the oval (design -3.6..-4.2): met. The bed derived from S1-S1 under the oval centreline (+1.50 - 2.70 - 1.70 - 0.50 = -3.4) is 0.2 m above the printed range, inside the +-0.4 m.\n* HRPP576 Fig 2 contours (`A1`, `A7`), after the D6 fix (3.3): at the traced points outside the reef zone the model minus the contour level is 0.00 m (sd 0.01, n 24) for -3.0 and 0.00 m (sd 0.00, n 13) for -4.0 (both are controls), and -0.14 m (sd 0.04, n 24) for -2.0 (not a control; unchanged by the fix). **Before the fix the model was 0.89 m deeper than the -4.0 contour** (sd 0.14, n 13): the anchor -4.0 at y = 335 m plus the -1.1 % gradient gave -4.7 mODN at y 400 m and -5.1 at y 440, where Fig 2 has -4.0 at y 408-460, and the nodes with y > 335 m (design bed under the arm head) were ignored; the volumes changed by -3.4 to -3.9 % when this was corrected (3.3). The check against the bridge and extension nodes is not independent (they are assumptions). Navionics (implied zero -3.58 from the Fig 2 -4.0 contour, -3.75 from the model after the fix, -4.73 before) and EMODnet cells (-3.07) are still shallower than the model, i.e. two independent sources still disagree with it by 0.7-1.3 m offshore; Fig 2 (the published contour figure of the physical-modelling report) is the reference that was used.\n* Beach (`A7`): the 2022 LiDAR DTM has a salient (contours pushed offshore) behind the mounds at x -20..+100 m: its -2.0 contour is at y about 245 m there against about 205 m elsewhere and in Fig 2 (survey about 12 years older). The measured 2022 DTM is used there.\n* EMODnet absolute cells are 0.7-1.2 m shallower than the model at y 368-513 (mean 0.87 m; 1.5-1.6 m before the D6 fix; known: the reef-zone cells are GEBCO interpolation); only the gradient is used.\n\n### 4.5 Registry rows closed (STEP 0)\n\n| id | what it bore on | result |\n|---|---|---|\n| img-14, img-15, img-16 (EMODnet viewer, sources, profile) | seabed, tides | MSL - LAT = 2.75 m (E3); reef-zone cells 1.0-1.4 m too shallow versus the design bed (interpolated); only the gradient -1.1 % is used (E4, from the Fig 2 -4.0 contour since D6); the three images are kept as context |\n| img-17 (HRPP576 Fig 2) | seabed -3..-5 mODN | -3.0 and -4.0 contours are controls since D6 (model 0.00 m both); -2.0 -0.14 m; before D6: -4.0 was -0.89 m (model deeper; sections 3.3, 4.4) |\n| img-20 (drawing 1020) | planform, crest zones | rings, crest paths and SOP re-projected and used; design foot IoU 0.9998 |\n| img-21 (drawing 1021) | crest, layers, slopes | +0.50 / +1.00 / +0.00, ramps 40 m / 15 m, layer 2.70 m, toe berm confirmed; bed -4.0 derived |\n| img-22 (drawing 1022) | head slope, mid slope | N4 +0.50, 1:4 flanks, N5 / N6 +0.00 / -2.70 confirmed |\n| img-23 (drawing 1023) | oval crest, bed | +1.50, flat top 6 m, 1:3, printed bed range -4.2..-3.6 AOD; bed derived from S1 -3.4 |\n| img-24 (LiDAR DSM sections) | crest and flank shape | section 4.2: median +0.58 / +1.55 against +0.50 / +1.50; LiDAR minus design +0.14 +- 0.28 m |\n\n## 5. Uncertainty\n\nSimple (root-sum-square) propagation, independent inputs:\n\n```\nsigma(z_crest, design) = 0.1 m                      (drawing labels, 0.05 m measured on a 300 dpi render)\nsigma(z_crest, LiDAR)  = 0.15 m (vertical datum A4) + 0.1-0.3 m (block tops)\nsigma(z_bed under rock) = 0.4 m                     (A6: derived layer thicknesses; beds \"vary\")\nsigma(H = z_crest - z_bed) = sqrt(0.15^2 + 0.4^2) = 0.43 m  ->  north arm H = 0.50 + 4.0 (design bed; model mean under the foot ring 3.86) = 4.4-4.5 m +- 0.5; oval 1.50 + 3.95 = 5.5 m +- 0.5     (E7)\nsigma(z_MSL) = sqrt(0.15^2 [MSL offset] + 0.1^2) = 0.18 m  -> crest height above MSL +0.19 m (arm) is +-0.2 m\ncrest depth below the water, d = z_water - z_crest: sigma(d) = sqrt(0.18^2 + sigma_crest^2) = 0.2-0.3 m                                     (E8)\ndV = 0.4 m x A(footprint incl. toe) = 0.4 x 11,391 = 4,556 m3 (15 %); density 1.6-1.8 t/m3 -> +-6 %; edge-level choice: +-8 % (section 4.1)    (E9)\n```\n\nPosition: +-5 m (Esri), +-2-5 m (Helmert); horizontal seabed contours +-10 m. The sea floor outside the reef is less certain than under it: it follows the Fig 2 -3.0 and -4.0 contours (+-0.5 m, +-10 m) and, beyond the -4.0 contour, the EMODnet gradient; at y 370-515 m it is 0.7-1.2 m deeper than the EMODnet cells and about 1.3 m deeper than the Navionics chart zero (LAT) implies (section 4.4; before the D6 fix 0.9 m deeper than Fig 2 itself). Vertical exaggeration k multiplies every height and every slope in the picture by k while plan distances stay true; at k = 5 the 1:4 flanks look like 1:0.8. Heights, slopes and tide read-outs on screen are always true values (divided by k); the photo-match mode forces k = 1.\n\n## 6. Confidence\n\n**MEDIUM.** Against the project rubric (high = every input sourced and cross-checked within about 10 %, medium = key inputs sourced but at least one first-order input estimated, low = a first-order input missing): the plan is high (design rings and laser survey agree to 1 pixel / 0.3 m), the crest levels are high (three independent readings: design drawing, LiDAR, section drawings), the tide offsets are medium (MSL is a four-level estimate +-0.15 m, LAT from neighbouring ports), and the seabed is medium-low: under the rock it is the drawing-derived -4.0 mODN (+-0.4 m), around the rock a hand-read contour figure (-3.0 and -4.0 contours are controls since D6), and seaward of the -4.0 contour (y 408-473 m) an EMODnet gradient that sits 0.7-1.2 m deeper than the EMODnet cells themselves. Because the first-order unknown for \"height above the bed\" and for the volume is the bed level, the overall level is medium.\n\n## 7. Limitations and unknowns\n\n| gap | effect on the model | what would resolve it | Lior could supply |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| bed level under the rock (\"EXISTING SEABED VARIES\"; no survey) | reef height +-0.5 m, volume +-15 % | post-construction multibeam or the Phase 1 as-built; Ceredigion beach-profile monitoring | Navionics app: spot depths at the reef toe (REQUESTS_FOR_LIOR.md) |\n| **resolved 2026-10-07 (D6):** the sea floor seaward of the reef was 0.89 m deeper than the Fig 2 -4.0 contour; the contour is now a control (3.3). Remaining: the -4.0 level is bridged / extended by assumption at x -104..170 and x > 170, and the model is 0.7-1.2 m deeper than the EMODnet cells | visible sea floor and depth read-outs at y > 335 m (+-0.5 m) | a sounding line or multibeam along y 335-520 m | Navionics / Google Earth depth readings along y 335-520 m |\n| HAT not found; MSL and LAT offsets for Aberystwyth not tabulated | slider top is MHWS; +-0.15 m on z_MSL | Admiralty Tide Tables / UKHO EasyTide Aberystwyth (Z0, HAT) | a tide-table reading |\n| LiDAR vertical datum not stated | +-0.15 m on crest heights | tile metadata | - |\n| flanks below the water edge unknown (LiDAR flown at about LAT, edge -2.3) | toe geometry and the lower part of the volume are design, not measured | a multibeam or drone survey at a spring low | Google Earth Pro historical imagery at a very low tide |\n| as-built plan, post-2022 state | later damage would not be modelled | Wales Coastal Monitoring Centre data, CoastSnap Borth photos | - |\n| Navionics shows neither the reef nor a consistent seabed | no crest cross-check; datum assumed | the Navionics app (SonarChart live) may show more than the web viewer | Navionics app reading at the listed coordinates |\n| rock density | tonnage +-6 % | quarry bulk-density data (Type 4 supply) | - |\n\n## 8. References\n\n* Blacka, M.J., Shand, T.D., Carley, J.T. and Mariani, A. (2013). A Review of Artificial Reefs for Coastal Protection in NSW. WRL Technical Report 2012/08, UNSW Water Research Laboratory. https://doi.org/10.4225/53/58e1dc7368c7a, p. 60, Fig 4.1 (registry img-31; context only, not a model input).\n* Garmin Ltd / Navionics (2026). Marine Maps web viewer, SonarChart and Nautical Charts. https://maps.garmin.com/en-US/marine, accessed 2026-10-07. Not to be used for navigation.\n* EMODnet Bathymetry Consortium (2024). EMODnet Digital Bathymetry (DTM 2024). https://doi.org/10.12770/cf51df64-56f9-4a99-b1aa-36b8d7b743a1. CC BY 4.0.\n* Esri (2012-2024). World Imagery (Wayback dates 2012-10-27, 2013-06-04, 2022-08-25, 2024-09-17). https://server.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/World_Imagery/MapServer.\n* Hansford, M. (Mark) (2011). Borth's Big Dig. New Civil Engineer, 15 September 2011. https://www.newcivilengineer.com/archive/borths-big-dig-15-09-2011/.\n* Haskoning UK Ltd (2010-11). Borth Coastal Protection Scheme - Phase 1, drawings 9V5090/1001, 1020, 1021, 1022, 1023 rev C1. Ceredigion County Council; published at borthcommunity.info.\n* National Tidal and Sea Level Facility (n.d.). Chart datum and ordnance datum. https://ntslf.org/tides/datum.\n* RCAHMW (2020). Coflein, Coastal Defences, Borth, NPRN 424699. https://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/424699.\n* Rigden, T., Stewart, T., Allsop, W. and Johnson, A. (2013). Surf reefs - physical modelling results, not pipe dreams. HR Wallingford report HRPP576; ICE Coasts, Marine Structures and Breakwaters, Edinburgh.\n* Royal HaskoningDHV (2011). West of Wales Shoreline Management Plan 2, Section 4 Coastal Area C Introduction, p. 4C.3 (report 9T9001).\n* Royal HaskoningDHV (2013). Ceredigion Beach Profile Monitoring Volume I, 2012-2013 survey, report 9W1559, section 2.15 Borth LiDAR.\n* Welsh Government (2022). LiDAR 2020-22, 1 m DSM/DTM, tile SN6089 (flown 19 March 2022). DataMapWales; Open Government Licence. https://datamap.gov.wales/.\n", "register_md": "# REGISTER_3D - sources, methods, assumptions, decisions, confidence (Borth coastal defence reef)\n\nSource audit of 2026-10-10 (Lior's standard: every statement has a source; every source says how it was used; methods, assumptions and decisions are explicit). This page is the one place that lists them; detail and numbers are in METHODS_3D.md (sections cited as \"M 3.3\" etc.), the Sources tab (provenance of every model value) and shape.json. Registry ids \"img-NN\" are rows of `03_images/reefs/borth-coastal-defence-reef/images.json` (31 rows). Not for navigation. All saved images and drawings are private research copies; reuse rights are not cleared.\n\n## R1. Source register - what was read, how, accuracy, which model element\n\n| id | full citation (accessed) | location used | what was read off it | how (method) | accuracy | model element / role |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| drg1020 | Haskoning UK Ltd for Ceredigion County Council (2010-11). Borth Coastal Protection Scheme - Phase 1, drawing 9V5090/1020 rev C1 \"Multi-Purpose Reef Plan\", 1:500, For Construction (Jan 2011). http://www.borthcommunity.info/index.php/planning-drawings/65-planning-drawings (2026-10-06); img-20 | plan sheet; 15 setting-out points (SOP) table | concentric rings 0-4 of the two mounds, SOP coordinates (OS grid), crest-strip paths | vector paths extracted from the PDF; affine fit of the PDF to the OS-grid SOP; OS grid to model frame by pyproj Helmert (M 3.1) | georeference residual 0.02 m; +-0.5 m on the drawing; +-2-5 m absolute (Helmert) | plan of version design_1020; design overlay on the trace; oval length (decision DEC11) |\n| drg1021-1023 | same set, drawings 9V5090/1021 (Northern Reef Sections 1/2, N1-N3, 1:200), /1022 (2/2, N4-N6), /1023 (Southern Reef Sections S1, S2); img-21..23 | section labels and dimension strings | crest +0.50 / +1.00 / +0.00 mODN, +1.50 oval; Type 4 layer 2.70 m; Type 5; Type 6 0.5 m blanket; toe berm 1.35 m high, 3.0 m top, 1:1.5; apron 2.0 m; slopes 1:3, 1:4, 1:5; transitions 40 m and 15 m; printed bed note \"-4.2 TO -3.6m AOD\"; MHWS +2.56 / MLWS -1.74 | read from the vector PDF text and dimensions, also by eye on 300 dpi renders | labels +-0.1 m; 0.05 m measured on render | crest levels, layers, toe, slopes, bed under the rock (derived: 2.70 + 1.31 + 0.50 below +0.50 = -4.01) |\n| drg1001 | same set, 9V5090/1001 General Arrangement 1:2000; img-19 | whole sheet | layout that was built (two separate mounds) | by eye | - | context only |\n| lidar2022 | Welsh Government (2022). LiDAR 2020-22, 1 m DSM and DTM, tile SN6089, flown 2022-03-19 03:15-03:20 UTC; DataMapWales, Open Government Licence v3. https://dmwproductionblob.blob.core.windows.net/lidar-zips/2020-22/dsm/wg_del_29_260289_20220319dsm.tif (DTM: same path with dtm) (2026-10-06); img-24 | tile SN6089, EPSG:27700, 1 m | DSM = as-built exposed rock surface; DTM = beach and foreshore; sea surface at the flight (-2.2..-2.4 mODN); crest and slope statistics | cells with a return = outline (1 m, simplified 0.4 m); DSM smoothed (Gaussian sigma 0.8 m, A9); DTM sampled on a 6 m grid as seabed nodes (rock buffered out 12 m); script `3d/prep_sources.py`, `3d/b3d_lib.py` | 1 m; vertical datum not stated (ODN assumed, A4); block tops 0.1-0.3 m above design surface | PRIMARY for the default surface (version lidar_2022), beach seabed (3,081 nodes), outline 8,675 m2, validation of crests and design |\n| hrpp576f2 | Rigden, T., Stewart, T., Allsop, W. and Johnson, A. (2013). Surf reefs - physical modelling results, not pipe dreams. HR Wallingford report HRPP576, ICE Coasts, Marine Structures and Breakwaters, Edinburgh, Sept 2013. https://eprints.hrwallingford.com/931/1/HRPP576_SurfReefs.pdf (2026-10-06); img-17 | p. 4, Figure 2 (849 x 349 px, 1.776 m/px from the 500 m scale bar) | mODN seabed contours -2, -3, -4 around the reefs; reef C (oval) position and role | contours traced by hand on the figure and georeferenced to the frame (`3d/src/fig2_contours_canonical.json`); -3.0 and -4.0 used as control nodes since D6 | +-0.5 m by eye, +-10 m horizontal | seabed outside the reef zone (12 + 13 nodes); -2.0 contour only a check |\n| hrpp576f3 | same paper, p. 4, Figure 3 \"Initial plan view layout and side profile of the Northern reef\"; img-18 | Fig 3 | superseded geotube design (truncated delta 180 x 65 m, 4.0 m high) | by eye | - | NOT used for geometry (superseded design); noted only |\n| sat2024 | Esri World Imagery (Esri, Maxar, Earthstar Geographics), image date 2024-09-17, zoom 19, 5.4995 px/m, centre 52.4832,-4.0562. https://server.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/World_Imagery/MapServer (fetched 2026-10-04 with `07_scale/tools/satellite.py`); img-08 | whole frame | visible bare-rock edge at low tide | hand tracing, 79 vertices (N 42, S 37); procedure in METHOD.md | +-5 m absolute (Esri), +-2 m edge | version sat_2024 (5,743 m2); canonical.polygons_m; waterline height at the traced edge (-1.3 mODN) read from the DSM |\n| sat2022, sat2012 | Esri Wayback release 34007 (image 2022-08-25) and 18358 (image 2012-10-27), same frame; img-09, img-10 | whole frame | independent traces (polys_2022_v1.json, polys_2012_v1.json) | hand tracing | 2012 image 5 m east of 2024 (registration, not movement) | cross-check only: no change 2012-2024 |\n| sat2013, satshore, satblobs | Esri Wayback 11351 (2013-06-04); Esri current 2024-09-17 frames r = 350 m and 250 m; img-11..13 | - | shoreline / defence line (back of the shingle beach); context | by eye | - | frame origin and offshore distance; not traced |\n| emodnet | EMODnet Bathymetry Consortium (2024). EMODnet Digital Bathymetry (DTM 2024). https://doi.org/10.12770/cf51df64-56f9-4a99-b1aa-36b8d7b743a1, CC BY 4.0; survey cells CDI 115084 (OceanWise Ltd) (2026-10-06); img-14..16 | map-viewer cell values and source layer | cross-shore gradient -1.1 % (soundings y 368-652 m) | read by eye from the viewer, linear fit of the surveyed cells (img-16) | +-0.3 % in slope | seabed beyond the Fig 2 -4.0 contour (equation E4); the absolute cells are GEBCO interpolation in the reef zone and are NOT used |\n| ref3 | Royal HaskoningDHV (2011). West of Wales Shoreline Management Plan 2, Section 4 Coastal Area C Introduction, p. 4C.3, water-level table, Aberystwyth row; report 9T9001, Final Nov 2011. https://www.grwparfordirolgorllewincymru.cymru/sites/default/files/2019-06/4c1%20-%20Section%204%20Coastal%20Area%20C%20Introduction.pdf (2026-10-06) | p. 4C.3 table | MHWS +2.56, MHWN +1.06, MLWN -0.64, MLWS -1.74 mAOD; extreme still water 10/50/100 yr +3.76 / +4.36 / +4.73 | tabulated | as printed | tide presets; MSL = mean of the four (+0.31, A2) |\n| ref4 | National Tidal and Sea Level Facility (n.d.). Chart datum and ordnance datum: differences at selected ports. https://ntslf.org/tides/datum (2026-10-06) | table, Barmouth and Fishguard rows | LAT = ODN - 2.44 m (Aberystwyth not listed) | tabulated; transferred to Aberystwyth/Borth (A3, A14) | +-0.1 m | LAT preset; EMODnet conversion E3 |\n| ref6 | Hansford, Mark (2011-09-15). Borth's Big Dig. New Civil Engineer. https://www.newcivilengineer.com/archive/borths-big-dig-15-09-2011/ (2026-10-06); first name from the reef card (R1 of 02_research/reefs/borth-coastal-defence-reef.json); the article could not be re-found by web search on 2026-10-10 | text | 275,000 t whole Phase 1; Type 4 (6-10 t) 42,000 t | quoted | whole scheme only | upper-bound check of the Type 4 volume; no model input |\n| ref7 | RCAHMW (2020). Coflein, Coastal Defences, Borth, NPRN 424699, record dated 2020-01-31. https://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/424699 (2026-10-06) | site record | completion 8 March 2012; about 300 m offshore; about 300,000 t whole works (quoting a BBC article of 8 March 2012) | quoted | - | state modelled (as-built final layout); offshore distance (300 m vs 400 m NCE: sources disagree) |\n| ref8, ref9 | Royal HaskoningDHV (2013). Ceredigion Beach Profile Monitoring Volume I, 2012-2013 survey, report 9W1559, section 2.15 (https://www.ceredigion.gov.uk/media/ke0fsyzz/ceredigion-beach-profile-monitoring-volume-2012-2013.pdf); Borth CPS Phase 2 public exhibition posters (2013), borthcommunity.info (2026-10-06) | text | Phase 1 complete; Phase 2 structures north of the reef | quoted | - | context (Phase 2 not modelled) |\n| img-31 | Blacka, M.J., Shand, T.D., Carley, J.T. and Mariani, A. (2013). A Review of Artificial Reefs for Coastal Protection in NSW. WRL Technical Report 2012/08, UNSW Water Research Laboratory. https://doi.org/10.4225/53/58e1dc7368c7a, p. 60, Fig 4.1 (2026-10-07) | Fig 4.1 | oblique photo June 2012 (source named there: eCoast 2012) | not traced | no scale | dated evidence that part of the reef is near the surface in June 2012; validation context only. NOT used by the 3D build; its dimensions for Borth (Table 4.2) describe the superseded two-reef design |\n| navionics | Garmin Ltd / Navionics (2026). Marine Maps web viewer, Nautical Charts and SonarChart. https://maps.garmin.com/en-US/marine (2026-10-07); img-26..29 | 44 screenshots, zoom 17 / 18 | depth contours | pixel rows read by `3d/nav_read.py`; datum test against seven datums (M 3.6) | datum +-0.6 m (not stated, A13) | cross-check of seabed and datum ONLY (rejected as input: shows no reef, 0.6-1.1 m shallower than Fig 2 offshore) |\n| photos | img-01..07: press/video photographs of the construction (Apr-Aug 2011) and video frames | - | qualitative construction state | by eye | - | page illustration only; not used for any number |\n| gemini | Gemini folder footprint for Borth (200 x 200 m, 12,500 m2, 110,000 t, Halcrow, ...) | VERIFY.md check 6 | - | compared with the original sources by the verifier | - | REJECTED: does not match drawings, LiDAR or imagery; nothing from it is used |\n\nNot found at a primary source (searched): HAT at Aberystwyth; a reef-only rock quantity; an as-built plan or post-construction survey of the reef; a Navionics depth at the reef.\n\n## R2. Methods that are not a direct reading (script paths relative to this folder)\n\n| method | where | parameters / equation | script |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| hand tracing of the rock edge | shape.json canonical; METHOD.md | 79 vertices on sat2024; 2022 and 2012 traced independently; IoU 0.84 (2024/2022) | by hand on the Esri tiles (METHOD.md); verification figures in `../overlays`, scripts in `../scripts_verify` |\n| georeference of drawing 1020 | M 3.1 | PDF similarity fit to 15 SOP; OS grid to WGS84 Helmert (pyproj) to local tangent plane to rotation; affine fit, residual rms 0.005 m | `b3d_lib.Frame`, `../scripts_verify/design1.py`, `design2.py`, `sop.py` |\n| datum conversion | M 3.1 | E1 z_MSL = z_ODN - 0.31; E2 z_LAT = z_ODN + 2.44; E3 z_MSL = e_LAT - 2.75 | `build_3d.py` TIDES_ODN |\n| LiDAR surface | M 3.2 | exposed-cell outline, DSM Gaussian smoothing sigma 0.8 m, floor at the edge level -2.3 mODN | `b3d_reef.py`, `prep_sources.py` |\n| design surface | M 3.2 | linear interpolation in the distance between the bounding rings (rings 0-4), then linear rise to the crest outline | `b3d_reef.py` |\n| toe below the water edge | M 3.2 | design toe slopes 1:1.5, 2 m flat apron, 0.5 m blanket, attached to the edge of the chosen version | `b3d_reef.py` |\n| seabed | M 3.3 | thin-plate-spline RBF (scipy RBFInterpolator, smoothing 0.5, 80 neighbours) through control nodes, 5 m grid; beyond the -4.0 contour E4 z = z_RBF(x, y_c) - 0.011 (y - y_c) | `b3d_lib.py` (`seabed_grid`, `contour4_line`) |\n| volumes | M 3.2 | integral of (surface - seabed) over the 1 m grid; tonnes = volume x 1.7 t/m3; uncertainty dV = 0.4 m x footprint incl. toe | `b3d_reef.py`, `build_3d.py` |\n| uncertainty propagation | M 5 | root-sum-square, independent inputs | M 5 equations E7-E9 |\n| Navionics reading and datum test | M 3.6 | pixel-row reading; best-of-7 datum by RMS | `nav_read.py`, `src/make_navionics_reading.py`, `src/navionics/capture_navionics.py` |\n| AI tools and earlier agents | - | The tracer, verifier and 3D agents are AI agents (Claude); their estimates were checked: outline against the design plan (IoU 0.79 / 0.86) and the LiDAR; crests against three independent readings; seabed against Fig 2 contours (M 4.4). Gemini output was not used (REJECTED above). | - |\n\n## R3. Assumptions (A1-A14; \"ASSUMPTION\" in the model means one of these)\n\n| id | assumed | why (evidence) | effect on results | would replace it |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A1 | the drawn design is the built design; the layout is the final one completed 8 Mar 2012 | no as-built plan published; ref7 date; LiDAR 2022 agrees with the crests (plan IoU 0.79 / 0.86, centroid 0.3-4 m) | surface above -2.3 mODN is measured (default version); below it the design is used; not quantified separately | as-built survey |\n| A2 | MSL = +0.31 mODN (+-0.15) = mean of MHWS, MHWN, MLWN, MLWS | no MSL printed at ref3 | +-0.15 m on every z_MSL height (crest +0.19 m MSL is +-0.2 m) | tide-table value for Aberystwyth/Borth (request row 6) |\n| A3 | LAT = -2.44 mODN (+-0.1) | Barmouth and Fishguard (ref4); Aberystwyth not listed; consistent with the LiDAR sea surface -2.2..-2.4 and with MHWS 5.00 m CD | LAT preset and EMODnet conversion (E3) +-0.1 m | Admiralty / UKHO Z0 for Aberystwyth |\n| A4 | LiDAR heights are ODN | not stated in the tile; agrees with design crests and the flight sea surface | +-0.15 m on LiDAR crest heights | tile metadata |\n| A5 | water level at the flight (LiDAR edge) = -2.3 mODN (+-0.1) | lowest returns -2.2..-2.4 | edge level of the default version; volume spread between versions about 15 % (25.5-30.1 kt m3) | tide prediction for 2022-03-19 03:15 UTC |\n| A6 | seabed under the rock: arm -4.0 (derived from layer thicknesses), tail -3.0..-3.4 (assumed), N4 -3.8, oval -3.6 / -4.2 (printed note; derived centreline -3.4) | drawing ground lines are schematic (\"EXISTING SEABED VARIES\") | +-0.4 m bed = +-4,556 m3 = +-15 % volume; height above bed +-0.5 m | post-construction multibeam or beach-profile survey |\n| A7 | -4.0 contour bridged at x -104..170 (6 nodes) and extended east of x 170 (3 nodes); EMODnet gradient -1.1 % beyond it | the figure hides the contour behind the drawn mounds; trend of the last 270 m (+0.146 m/m) | visible sea floor and depth read-outs at y > 335 m +-0.5 m; effect on rock volume not quantified (reef window ends at y 420 m) | sounding line or multibeam |\n| A8 | placed-rock bulk density 1.7 t/m3 (1.6-1.8) | solid 2.65 t/m3 with about 36 % voids; no quarry value | tonnage +-6 % | quarry bulk density of the Type 4 supply |\n| A9 | DSM smoothing sigma 0.8 m; DSM block tops are 0.1-0.3 m above the design surface | visual noise reduction; compared with design (+0.14 +- 0.28 m) | crest heights +0.1-0.3 m; volume effect not quantified | - |\n| A10 | edge levels of the versions: -2.3 (lidar_2022), -2.15 (design_1020), -1.3 (sat_2024) | -2.15 = bed -4.0 + 0.5 + 1.35; -1.3 = median DSM at the traced edge (north -1.25, south -1.36) | footprint 5,743-8,675 m2, volume 25.5-30.1 k m3 | - |\n| A11 | flanks below the water edge are the design toe (1:1.5, 2 m apron); as-built flanks above the edge are flatter (1:4.0-1:5.3) and 8-10 m wider | no survey below the edge | lower part of the volume is design, not measured; not quantified separately | multibeam or drone survey at a spring low |\n| A12 | still, horizontal water surface at each level | viewer simplification | none on geometry; no wave set-up or run-up shown | - |\n| A13 | Navionics chart datum is LAT | UK convention; best of 7 datums by RMS | none on the model (cross-check only) | Navionics app / chart legend |\n| A14 | Aberystwyth tide levels (ref3) and the Barmouth/Fishguard LAT offset (ref4) apply at Borth | nearest tabulated port; Borth values not tabulated | feeds A2, A3 (+-0.15 / +-0.1 m); added by the audit of 2026-10-10 to make an implicit assumption explicit | tide table for Borth |\n\n## R4. Decisions (who, when, options, reason)\n\n| id | decision | options | who / when | reason |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| DEC1 | model the as-built final layout; the oval is a shore-parallel breakwater, not a surf reef | superseded two-geotube design; final layout | orchestrator, 2026-10-06 (SOURCES_3D.md) | Coflein completion 8 Mar 2012 (ref7); drawings 1020-1023 rev C1 |\n| DEC2 | canonical frame +x south, +y offshore (west) | tracer's +x north (mirror image) | verifier, 2026-10-06 | the tracer's frame had x pointing north, a mirror image of the shoreline geometry (HANDOFF section 2); do not flip back |\n| DEC3 | primary trace = Esri 2024-09-17 | 2012, 2013, 2022 images | tracer / verifier, 2026-10-05/06 | bare rock at low tide, sharpest edge; others kept as cross-checks |\n| DEC4 | default outline = laser survey (lidar_2022); design_1020 and sat_2024 are toggles | priority survey > drawing > photo | Lior, 2026-10-06 | a laser survey is more accurate than a photo |\n| DEC5 | working datum ODN; model zero = MSL (+0.31 mODN) | ODN zero; LAT zero | orchestrator, 2026-10-06 | fixed in SOURCES_3D.md 'Decisions fixed by the orchestrator': z = 0 at MSL, local tides only (Lior's rule 5) |\n| DEC6 | tide slider ends at MHWS; no Haifa preset | extend to HAT | agent with orchestrator, 2026-10-06; Lior's rule \"local tides only\" | HAT not found at a primary source (5.9 m CD is a predicted maximum) |\n| DEC7 | D6: seabed seaward of the reef follows the Fig 2 -4.0 contour (option B, faithful fix) | A: freeze the old bed under the rock to keep the volumes; B: fix the clamp that ignored nodes with y > 335 m | coordinator, 2026-10-07 (Lior's D6 in REQUESTS_FOR_LIOR.md) | option A keeps a 0.7 m pit under the arm head; volumes fall 3.4-3.9 % (documented in M 3.3) |\n| DEC8 | EMODnet absolute cells not used in the reef zone | use them | 3D agent, 2026-10-06 | GEBCO interpolation, 1.0-1.4 m too shallow at the reef |\n| DEC9 | Navionics is a cross-check only | use as a seabed input | 3D agent, 2026-10-07 | no reef shown, datum not stated, 0.6-1.1 m shallower than Fig 2 offshore |\n| DEC10 | Gemini footprint rejected | - | verifier, 2026-10-06 (VERIFY check 6) | does not match the original sources |\n| DEC11 | oval crest length 38 m (SOP table of drawing 1020) | 46 m (S1-S1 dimensions on drawing 1023) | 3D agent, 2026-10-06 | plan georeferenced from the SOP table; the choice is worth about 2 % of the oval volume. OPEN: main/Lior may prefer the sections |\n| DEC12 | LiDAR heights taken as ODN | unknown datum | 3D agent, 2026-10-06 | see A4 |\n| DEC13 | toe below the water edge = design toe | none measured | 3D agent, 2026-10-06 | see A11 |\n| DEC14 | rock tonnage reported with a range and compared with NCE 42,000 t only as an upper bound | quote NCE as the reef quantity | 3D agent, 2026-10-06 | NCE figure covers the whole Phase 1 |\n\n## R5. Confidence per element\n\n| element | level | reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| plan | HIGH | design rings and laser survey agree to 1 pixel / 0.3 m; four dated images; absolute position +-5 m |\n| crest levels | HIGH | three independent readings: drawings, LiDAR (median +0.58 arm, +1.55 oval), sections; +-0.15-0.3 m |\n| tides / datum | MEDIUM | MSL a four-level mean (+-0.15 m); LAT from neighbouring ports; LiDAR datum assumed |\n| seabed | MEDIUM-LOW | under the rock a derived design bed (+-0.4 m); outside it hand-read contours (+-0.5 m, +-10 m) and, beyond them, an EMODnet gradient 0.7-1.2 m deeper than the EMODnet cells |\n| volume / tonnage | MEDIUM | +-15 % from the bed, +-6 % density, version spread; no reef-only quantity exists to check it |\n| overall | MEDIUM | the bed level is the first-order unknown for height and volume |\n\n## R6. Consistency notes of the audit (details in SOURCE_AUDIT_2026-10-10.md)\n\n* The registry has 31 rows (row 31 added 2026-10-10 from Blacka et al. 2013); older text says 30.\n* LiDAR crest statistics depend on the cell mask: north arm median +0.58 / p90 +0.91 (this model, M 4.2), median +0.69 and p90 +0.72..+0.88 (verifier, VERIFY.md, img-24). Different masks, not a conflict; the model uses neither as input (crests are the design values).\n* Whole-Phase-1 tonnage: NCE 275,000 t (ref6) against Coflein \"about 300,000 t\" (ref7); the model uses neither except as an upper-bound comparison.\n* Offshore distance: 300 m (Coflein) against 400 m (NCE); the model uses the georeferenced position (reef centroid 330.5 m from the defence line).\n", "sources_md": "# SOURCES_3D - borth-coastal-defence-reef\n\nRun started 2026-10-06 (3D agent, Sonnet 5.5). Brief: _agent_briefs\\model_3d.md. Inputs: HANDOFF.md (verifier, 2026-10-06).\n\n## Decisions fixed by the orchestrator (2026-10-06)\n- STATE modelled: as-built final layout, Phase 1 completed 8 March 2012 (Coflein ref7). Two mounds: N boot-shaped surf reef (crest +0.50 mODN, tail +1.00, head edge +0.00 rising to +0.50) and S oval SHORE-PARALLEL BREAKWATER (crest +1.50 mODN; not a surf reef). Superseded two-geotube design = note only.\n- FRAME: canonical frame of shape.json (+x south 179.57 deg, +y west/offshore); z = 0 at MSL; z_MSL = z_ODN - 0.31.\n- Tides (mODN): LAT -2.44 (chart datum), MLWS -1.74, MLWN -0.64, MHWN +1.06, MHWS +2.56.\n- Reef surface: Royal Haskoning DRG 9V5090/1020-1023 rev C1; toe at design armour foot (6,989 m2); validate vs 2022-03-19 LiDAR and the visible-rock trace (5,743 m2).\n- Seabed: -4.0 mODN under N arm, -3.6..-4.2 mAOD under oval, HRPP576 Fig 2 contours (-3..-5 mODN) around; beyond the Fig 2 -4.0 contour (y 408-473 m; before the D6 fix of 2026-10-07: beyond 335 m) the EMODnet trend only (z_MSL = e_LAT - 2.75).\n\n## Checkpoint log\n- 2026-10-06 start: 3d\\ created; nothing built yet.\n\n## UPDATE 2026-10-06 (orchestrator message, replaces \"toe at design armour foot\")\nLior chose the DEFAULT edge = laser survey. Three outline versions (ids): lidar_2022 (DEFAULT, 8,675 m2, \"Laser survey edge (Welsh Government LiDAR, 19 Mar 2022)\"), design_1020 (\"Design rock-layer foot (Royal Haskoning drg 9V5090/1020 rev C1, Jan 2011)\", 6,989 m2), sat_2024 (\"Visible rock edge (Esri satellite photo, 17 Sep 2024)\", 5,743 m2).\nDefault construction: LiDAR DSM surface above the exposed level (about -2.3 mODN at the flight) + design slopes/toe berm of drawings 1021-1023 below it down to the seabed. Versions go into shape.json (outline_versions, default_outline \"lidar_2022\", versions_info), model.js (versions, default_version, versions_info), viewer selector with (i) pop-up, URL hash #version=<id>, METHODS_3D.md section 4 comparison. common.md rule 9: stop at ~60 tool calls / 300k tokens with \"HANDOFF_STOP:\".\n\n## Findings so far (agent run 1, before building)\n- Shared scratchpad of the verification still exists: C:\\Users\\lior\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--\\cd973dbd-a674-4cd5-adf1-ffb4c361545f\\scratchpad\\borth_verify\\ (design_fit.pkl = BNG->PDF similarity fit of drawing 1020; design_rings.pkl = 5 concentric rings N and S in BNG; lidar_polys.pkl; dl\\wg_dsm_SN6089.tif, wg_dtm_SN6089.tif (1 MB each, WG LiDAR 2022-03-19, 1 m, EPSG:27700, origin 260000/290000 top-left); dl\\HRPP576.pdf etc.). If gone: re-download tile (URL in HANDOFF 3.4).\n- Drawing reading (viewed 1020, 1021, 1022 at 100 dpi; vector PDFs in ..\\src): the plan is drawn with NORTH TO THE RIGHT (up = west = offshore). Ring order from outside in: ring0 apron outer edge at seabed, +0.76 ring1 (blanket top edge, blanket 0.5 m thick at 1:1.5), +2.0 ring2 (foot of toe-berm slope? = apron 2.0 m flat), +2.0 ring3 (toe-berm top outer edge, berm 1.35 m high at 1:1.5 = 2.0 m run), +3.0 ring4 (berm top inner edge = FOOT OF THE TYPE 4 ARMOUR SLOPE at about -2.15..-2.2 mODN) = alt_outlines_m armour_foot_design.\n- Section 1021 N1-N1 (longitudinal): from SOP R1 (+0.00, head) the crest ridge rises over 'TRANSITION APPROX. 40m' to +0.50; N1-N1 (second): from SOP R2 (+0.50) 'TRANSITION APPROX. 15m' to +1.00 at SOP R3 (tail), then 1:3 down to the toe. Type 4 layer 2.70 m thick (top +0.50 -> -2.20; head +0.00 -> -2.70; tail +1.00 -> -1.70). 1022 N5-N5 / N6-N6: head slopes 1:5 from the toe berm up to +0.00 at SOP R1 / R10; N4-N4 1:4 flanks, crest +0.50 between 'setting out curve' and 'change of direction'. In the plan the light-grey head lens is bounded by R1, R10, R9, R8, R7, R6 and an arc R1->R6' (R = 220 m, about 40 m long) = the rising ridge; the dark crest strip (9 m) starts at the line R6'-R6 and runs to R2/R5; tail strip R2/R5 -> R3/R4 (26.6 m) rises to +1.00. Slope labels on plan: 1:5 (head, left), 1:4 (right side of head/mid), 1:3 (arm), toe 1:1.5.\n- HAT: searched again (NTSLF datum table, UKHO-derived harbour pages): NOT found at a primary source. Secondary (visitmyharbour.com, UKHO-derived) confirms Aberystwyth MHWS 5.0 / MHWN 3.5 / MLWN 1.8 / MLWS 0.7 m CD -> MSL = 2.75 m CD, consistent with -2.44 mODN LAT. tide-forecast.com listing max predicted high 5.9 m CD (not HAT). DECISION: slider top = MHWS (+2.56 mODN = +2.25 m MSL).\n- Reef volume / tonnage: searched (NCE 'Borth's Big Dig' 2011 = whole scheme 275,000 t incl. Type 4 6-10 t 42,000 t; BBC 8 Mar 2012 'nearly 300,000 t' whole scheme; Coflein ref7; borthcommunity.info; HRPP576; phase2 posters): NO reef-only volume at a primary source. -> report model volume and implied tonnage at stated density; compare Type-4 volume to NCE 42,000 t as an upper-bound check (that figure also covers breakwaters/groynes).\n\n## RUN 2 (3D agent, continuation, 2026-10-06) - checkpoint log\n- Re-read: common.md, model_3d.md, HANDOFF.md, this file. Code so far: 3d\\b3d_lib.py (frame BNG<->canonical, affine fit rms 0.005 m, design foot ring IoU 0.9998 vs shape.json alt_outlines), 3d\\prep_sources.py.\n- LiDAR source files: 3d\\src\\lidar_window_SN6089_2022-03-19.npz (reef window, DSM+DTM, by run 1) and 3d\\src\\lidar_beach_SN6089_2022-03-19.npz (E 260300-260900, N 289000-289500, DSM+DTM values; the .tif tile itself is not kept; URL in HANDOFF 3.4).\n- Design extraction by run 1: 3d\\src\\design1020_rings_bng.json (5 rings N, S), design1020_crest_paths_bng.json (crest ring edges R1-R6), design1020_pdf_to_bng_fit.json.\n- HRPP576 Fig 2 (img-17) read: north arrow wedge points LEFT (north = left; view from the sea). Contours: left labels -1.0..-5.0 mODN; tracking at column 330 (orig px) gave the grey lines at rows 139 (-2.0), 191 (-3.0), 262 (-4.0). Georeferenced via the oval C centre and the 500 m bar (1.776 m/px). Result: -3.0 contour at y about 287 m offshore at the reef (x -80..+120), -2.0 at about 208 m, -4.0 at about 410-425 m. 3d\\src\\fig2_contours_canonical.json. (+-10 m horizontally.)\n- Drawing 1021 viewed (sections N1-N1, N2-N2, N3-N3): ground line is a flat schematic ('EXISTING SEABED VARIES', note 4 'BED LEVELS VARY'); the -4.0 mODN under the N arm is derived from the drawn layer thicknesses (Type 4 2.7 m + Type 5 1.31 m + Type 6 0.5 m below +0.50 = -4.01), i.e. a typical design bed level, not a survey.\n- BUILD DATA DONE (first build; seabed and volumes superseded by the D6 entry at the end of this log) (build_3d.py -> model.js 656 kB, docs.js). Seabed = RBF (thin-plate) through ~3,100 nodes: LiDAR DTM beach (2022), Fig 2 -3.0 contour outside the reef zone, design bed nodes (arm -4.0, N4 -3.8, tail assumed -3.0..-3.4, oval -3.6 shoreward / -4.2 seaward), anchor line -4.0 at y=335 outside the reef x range; beyond y=335 EMODnet gradient -1.1 % from the modelled level. Model bed under N foot mean -4.04 (-4.72..-2.51), under oval -3.95 (-4.21..-3.62) (design -4.0 / -3.6..-4.2: met). Model at y=287: -3.61 (x=-60), -2.91 (x=85) vs Fig 2 -3.0 contour (ok). EMODnet absolute cells are 1.5-1.6 m shallower than the model at y=368-513 (known GEBCO-fill problem, not used).\n- Beach finding: the LiDAR beach has a salient (contours pushed offshore) at x about -20..+100, y 100-270 behind the reefs, which Fig 2 (pre-construction survey) does not show; measured 2022 data used.\n- Versions built (1 m grid): lidar_2022 31,336 m3 (53,300 t at 1.7 t/m3), design_1020 30,091 m3 (51,200 t), sat_2024 26,400 m3 (44,900 t); Type 4 layer 21.6k / 18.0k / 15.4k m3 = 36.6k / 30.6k / 26.1k t, all below NCE's 42,000 t (whole Phase 1 incl. other structures) = consistent upper bound.\n- LiDAR DSM crest: N arm median +0.58 / p90 +0.91; oval median +1.55 / p90 +1.75 (design +0.50 / +1.50). LiDAR minus design on common cells above -2.0 mODN: mean +0.14, sd 0.28 m.\n- DSM height along the traced 2024 rock edge: median -1.25 (N) / -1.36 (S) mODN -> the photo waterline, used as edge level of version sat_2024 (-1.3).\n- 2026-10-07 resume check (coordinator message): all files in 3d\\ are complete (b3d_lib.py, b3d_reef.py, prep_sources.py, build_3d.py parse; model.js 656 kB, docs.js, index.html 37 kB, src\\*). Nothing half-written. Still to do: viewer layout fixes + previews, Navionics reading, annotated images (Fig 2, drawings, LiDAR/beach, seabed map), METHODS_3D.md, REQUESTS_FOR_LIOR.md, registry rows (Step 0: img-14,15,16,17,20,21,22,23,24), shape.json outline_versions (python build_3d.py --shape), VERIFY.md line, HANDOFF.md, 05_qa\\00_STATUS.md.\n- Headless viewer test works: local python http.server on a random port 88xx + Chrome --headless=new (swiftshader) with a fresh --user-data-dir, launched with PowerShell Start-Process -Wait (bash path quoting failed for --screenshot).\n\n## RUN 3 (3D agent, finish run, 2026-10-07) - checkpoint log\n- STEP 1 DONE (viewer fixes). index.html: header is now a fixed grid (title | selector + (i) | confidence on row 1, state line on row 2, single-line ellipsis with full text on hover; layout is flex-column so nothing overflows the viewport); label declutter (priority: reef roles > north > crest > misc > scale bars > axis; a lower-priority label is hidden when it would overlap a higher one); oval labelled \"BREAKWATER (oval, not a surf reef)\" (red outline style), north mound \"SURF REEF (northern, boot-shaped)\" (labels come from build_3d.py roles; rebuilt model.js, only roles + crest_labels changed); tide slider LAT -2.75 .. MHWS +2.25 m MSL with a visible \"Why the slider stops at MHWS\" note (HAT not found at a primary source; 5.9 m CD is a predicted maximum); version selector (names with dates, [default] marker), (i) pop-up with versions_info + table (name | date | source | footprint | volume | edge level), #version=<id> hash (also written back on change), #info=1 opens the pop-up (used for tests); oblique preset moved closer, plan preset height 420 m.\n- Rendered preview_plan.png and preview_oblique.png (1400x900) with own headless Chrome (python http.server on a random free port, fresh --user-data-dir under %TEMP%, --headless=new --use-angle=swiftshader --virtual-time-budget=15000; helper in the session scratchpad render.py; the first load occasionally fails (three.js CDN race): re-run if the screenshot shows an empty panel). Viewed downscaled: reef, seabed, water, labels and scale OK; #version=sat_2024 and the (i) pop-up checked in a separate render.\n- STEP 3 (partly) DONE, 2026-10-07: annotated\\ made by make_annotations.py (new, this run; regenerates all A* images): A1_fig2_contours_read.png (HRPP576 Fig 2: -2/-3/-4 contours traced, printed labels boxed, column-330 read-out rows 139/191/262.5, 500 m bar and oval C centre, 1.776 m/px), A2_drg1020_rings_and_sop_read.png (drawing 1020 plan: vector rings 0-4, crest paths, SOP R1-R15 re-projected with the PDF<->OS fit; overlay agrees with the drawn rings to a pixel), A3_drg1021_sections_read.png, A4_drg1022_sections_read.png, A5_drg1023_sections_read.png (numbered callouts: crest +0.50/+1.00/+0.00/+1.50, underside levels, transitions 40 m / 15 m, 1:3 / 1:4 slopes, toe berm 1350, printed MHWS +2.56 / MLWS -1.74, bed note \"-4.2 TO -3.6m AOD\"), A6_lidar_crest_check.png (LiDAR DSM map + crest profiles vs design: N arm median +0.58 / p90 +0.91 vs +0.50, oval +1.55 / +1.75 vs +1.50), A7_lidar_beach_vs_fig2.png (2022 DTM contours vs Fig 2 -2/-3 contours: the beach salient behind the mounds, -2.0 contour at y about 245 m instead of about 205 m), A8_seabed_source_zones.png (control nodes by source + modelled contours; weak zones: tail bed assumed, anchor -4.0 at y = 335 assumed, nothing between y 335 and 520 but the EMODnet gradient).\n- New derived check from drawing 1023 S1-S1 (read in A5): +1.50 top, Type 4 underside -1.20, Type 5 underside -2.90, Type 6 0.5 m below => bed -3.4 mODN under the oval centreline (derived); the printed note on S2-S2 says \"-4.2 TO -3.6m AOD\" (model uses -3.6 shoreward / -4.2 seaward): derived value 0.2 m shallower than the printed range, inside the stated +-0.4 m.\n- Beach finding for METHODS: the LiDAR DTM water edge (flight at about LAT) is at y about 245 m (blank cells start at y 245-250 at x +150..+200, -2.2 mODN). \n- Navionics: capture_navionics.py (copy of the Palm Beach script, own headless Chrome, random free port, fresh profile; centre 52.483453 N, 4.056205 W; geohash gcm4edq678dp) run twice: test + full sweep (SonarChart + Nautical charts, zoom 17 and 18, Depth units = metres, Shallow shading 0..10 m). No login / CAPTCHA / consent dialog appeared. Files in 3d\\src\\navionics\\.\n- STEP 2 DONE (Navionics reading), 2026-10-07. Access: https://maps.garmin.com/en-US/marine?maps=another-brand&overlay=false&key=gcm4edq678dp (webapp.navionics.com successor; no login, CAPTCHA or consent dialog; nothing bypassed). Own headless Chrome (random free port, fresh profile, killed by PID afterwards), src\\navionics\\capture_navionics.py: layers SonarChart Maps and Nautical Charts, units metres, zoom 17 and 18, Shallow shading 0..10 m (44 screenshots, capture_log.json); geometry read from the page (probe_geometry.py -> nav_geometry.json: container 982 x 655 px, centre pixel (491, 327) = 52.483453 N, 4.056205 W; Mercator m/px = 156543.034 / 2^z, 0.730 m/px at z17). Credit \"Garmin Navionics, not for navigation\"; private research copy, reuse not cleared; chart data dates are not shown by the viewer.\n- READING (nav_read.py, results src\\navionics\\nav_lines.json, nav_datum_test.json, src\\navionics_reading.json): contours drawn at 0 (the edge of the green drying area), 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2, 2.5 m (printed labels 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2, 2.5 confirm the 0.5 m interval); along the beach they run shore-parallel; the 0 m line is at y = 385 (x -56) ... 430 (x +200) m offshore, 0.5 m at +45 m, 1 m at +105, 1.5 m at +163, 2 m at +212, 2.5 m at +293 m (z17). Nautical Chart: only 0 and 2 m lines plus one drying-height label (0.2 m, underlined, at canonical x -108.5, y 248). Datum NOT stated by the viewer; contour interval not stated (read as above).\n- DATUM TEST by RMS (zero level of the chart, mODN, implied by each reference = z_ref + d): model seabed -4.73 +- 0.20 (n 24, lines y <= 515); HRPP576 Fig 2 -4.0 contour -3.58 +- 0.07 (n 7); EMODnet cells at x -31 -3.07 +- 0.08 (n 3). RMS of the seven candidate datums (LAT -2.44, MLWS -1.74, MLWN -0.64, MSL +0.31, ODN 0, MHWN +1.06, MHWS +2.56): vs Fig 2 -4.0: 1.15 / 1.84 / 2.94 / 3.89 / 3.58 / 4.64 / 6.14 m; vs EMODnet 0.64 / 1.34 / 2.43 / 3.38 / 3.07 / 4.13 / 5.63; vs model 2.30 / 3.00 / 4.10 / 5.05 / 4.74 / 5.79 / 7.29. LAT is the best standard datum in all three references (assumed = UK chart datum), but no datum fits: offshore the chart is 1.1 m shallower than Fig 2 and 0.6 m shallower than EMODnet (and 2.3 m shallower than the model, see below). The one drying-height label (0.2 m above chart datum, x -108, y 248) = -2.24 mODN if LAT, and the LiDAR (sea at -2.3 mODN) has water there: consistent within 0.1 m.\n- CREST/HEIGHT CROSS-CHECK: structure still exists (LiDAR 2022, imagery to 2024) but is NOT visible in either layer: the mounds (0 to +1.5 mODN = 2.4-4 m above LAT) lie inside the uniform green drying area; no symbol or label; no feature at the footprint. The only charted shoal (closed 0.5 / 1 m contours) is at canonical (37, 405), 35 m seaward of the nearest rock and not the reef. => no use for crest depth or reef height; Navionics = seabed cross-check only (A9, A10).\n- NEW VALIDATION FINDING (not in earlier runs; RESOLVED by the D6 fix, see the last entry; the statement 'volumes are NOT affected' below turned out to be wrong for y > 335 m): the modelled seabed seaward of the reef is DEEPER than the Fig 2 -4.0 contour: model minus contour level +-: at the traced -4.0 contour points outside the reef zone the model is 0.89 m deeper (sd 0.14, n 13); -3.0 contour 0.01 m (n 24, used as control), -2.0 contour 0.14 m deeper (n 24, not a control). Cause: seabed_nodes uses only the Fig 2 -3.0 contour and an assumed anchor -4.0 at y = 335, then the EMODnet gradient -1.1 % from there (model -4.7 at y 400, -5.1 at 440); Fig 2 has -4.0 at y 410-450. Under the footprints the design bed nodes pin the bed, so the volumes are NOT affected; the visible sea floor in front of the reef is. Navionics (-3.6 implied zero) and EMODnet (-3.1) are both shallower than the model. NOT changed in this run (orchestrator decision of 2026-10-06 fixed the -4.0 anchor and the EMODnet trend); reported in METHODS_3D 4/7, HANDOFF and REQUESTS_FOR_LIOR.\n- Annotated: A9_navionics_sonarchart_z17_annotated.png (grid, outlines, contour crossings 0-2.5 m, Fig 2 contours, shoal, drying-height label, datum numbers), A10_navionics_nautical_z18_reef_outline.png.\n- STEP 4 DONE, 2026-10-07: python build_3d.py --shape wrote shape.json outline_versions (3), default_outline \"lidar_2022\", versions_info, outline_versions_chosen; checked: ids, names, dates, areas (8,675 / 6,989 / 5,743 m2), polygons_m identical to model.js versions[].outline_m, versions_info identical, canonical unchanged (compared with a copy before the write). VERIFY.md line added (\"Lior chose the LiDAR default, 2026-10-06\"). model.js rebuilt with the Navionics provenance row (navionics_reading.json), model-minus-Fig-2 statistics (-2: -0.14 m, -3: -0.01, -4: -0.89 m deeper, n 24/24/13) and a provenance row for the oval bed derived from S1-S1 (-3.4 mODN).\n- STEP 5 DONE, 2026-10-07: METHODS_3D.md (8 sections; section 3.6 Navionics and datum test; section 4.1 compares the three versions; 4.4 seabed checks incl. the new -0.89 m finding; 4.5 registry results), REQUESTS_FOR_LIOR.md (7 rows; Navionics app and Google Earth Pro points with lat/lon computed through the frame), docs.js rebuilt by build_3d.py (methods + this file), viewer tabs Methods / Sources show them.\n- Statement checked and softened: drawing 1023 S1-S1 dimensions the oval 23,000 + 23,000 mm between SOP R15 and R14 (46 m) while the SOP table of drawing 1020 gives R14-R15 = 38 m (BNG 289162 -> 289200); the plan strip measures 38 m; model uses the table; reported in METHODS 4.3, REQUESTS row 7, registry img-20.\n- STEP 6 DONE, 2026-10-07: register_images.py (3d\\) updated the registry: new rows img-26..29 (Navionics series, 11 files each as group_files, annotated A9 / A10 attached), img-30 (A8 seabed source-zone map); pending rows img-14, 15, 16, 17, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24 closed (pending false, result lines, annotated_files A1-A7 attached); IMAGES.md and NEW_IMAGES_LOG.md updated; check_registry.py exit 0, Borth: 30 registered, pending_3d 0. (3d\\preview_*.png are not registered on purpose, README rule.)\n- STEP 7 DONE, 2026-10-07: HANDOFF.md rewritten (84 lines, sections 1-6 per common.md rule 8; supersedes the verifier's), 05_qa\\00_STATUS.md entry added, project-root REQUESTS_FOR_LIOR.md updated (Borth row in section 4, decision D6 offshore seabed fix). Final rebuild: python build_3d.py --shape (model.js, docs.js, shape.json consistent). Open: D6, Navionics-app / Google Earth readings, Aberystwyth Z0 / HAT.\n- D6 SEABED FIX DONE, 2026-10-07 (fresh agent; archived before editing: 3d\\archive\\pre_D6\\ = model.js, docs.js, b3d_lib.py, build_3d.py, METHODS_3D.md, index.html, SOURCES_3D.md, make_annotations.py, nav_read.py, register_images.py, src\\build_metrics.json, src\\navionics_reading.json, nav_datum_test.json, previews, A1 / A7-A10).\n  - Code: b3d_lib.py: anchor nodes removed; Fig 2 -4.0 contour added as nodes (13 traced + 6 ASSUMED bridge + 3 ASSUMED east extension); new `contour4_line()`; `seabed_grid(nodes, c4=...)` evaluates the thin-plate fit at `min(y, y_c(x))` (y_c = the -4.0 contour) and applies the EMODnet gradient beyond y_c(x) only. build_3d.py / make_annotations.py pass `c4=`; text rows of build_3d.py, make_annotations.py (A1 caption, A8 legend), register_images.py updated.\n  - Cause of the old numbers: the old fit clamped y to ANCHOR_Y = 335, so all nodes with y > 335 m (design bed under the arm head at y 360 / 385, oval seaward nodes) were ignored; the bed under the arm head fell to -4.7 mODN (design -4.0).\n  - Results: model minus Fig 2 contour: -4.0 -0.89 -> 0.00 m (n 13); -3.0 -0.01 -> 0.00 (n 24); -2.0 -0.14 -> -0.14 (n 24). Model minus EMODnet cells (y 368 / 440 / 513, x -30): -1.52 / -1.62 / -1.61 -> -1.16 / -0.73 / -0.72 m. Navionics implied chart zero (model) -4.73 -> -3.75 mODN (Fig 2: -3.58, EMODnet: -3.07); RMS vs LAT 2.30 -> 1.33 m. Bed under the north foot ring -4.04 (-4.72..-2.51) -> -3.86 (-4.05..-2.51); oval unchanged -3.95.\n  - Volumes (the bed under the arm head rose): lidar_2022 31,336 -> 30,119 m3 (53,271 -> 51,203 t); design_1020 30,091 -> 28,919 m3 (51,154 -> 49,162 t); sat_2024 26,400 -> 25,504 m3 (44,880 -> 43,357 t); Type 4 layer of the default 21,557 -> 21,220 m3 (36,646 -> 36,074 t). The brief's acceptance 'volumes unchanged' was replaced by the coordinator with 'volumes change only through the bed-node fix, documented' (option A, freezing the old bed under the rock, rejected: it keeps a 0.7 m pit under the arm head). Whole-Phase-1 stated quantities (NCE Type 4 42,000 t; 275,000 t total; Coflein 300,000 t) are upper bounds, not reef-only: default Type 4 layer 36,074 t is 14 % below 42,000 t (was 13 %).\n  - Rebuild order used: build_3d.py -> nav_read.py -> src\\make_navionics_reading.py -> build_3d.py -> nav_read.py annotate -> make_annotations.py -> (METHODS_3D.md edited) -> build_3d.py --shape -> register_images.py -> previews re-rendered (headless Chrome, SOURCES_3D STEP 1 recipe).\n"};
